1
00:00:09,000 --> 00:00:15,201
It began with eleven people in a hospital in Ravenna
and a doctor who did the right thing

2
00:00:15,221 --> 00:00:16,460
at the right hour.

3
00:00:16,859 --> 00:00:23,264
The presentation was wrong for the season and wrong
for the region: a rapid respiratory

4
00:00:23,284 --> 00:00:29,984
illness with a four-day incubation and a case fatality
that the first cluster put somewhere

5
00:00:30,004 --> 00:00:36,630
north of eleven per cent. The doctor, a
forty-one-year-old registrar named Chiara Bonetti,

6
00:00:36,650 --> 00:00:42,980
ran the samples on the machine in her laboratory —
an Umbrella field unit, of the kind

7
00:00:43,000 --> 00:00:49,478
given away at cost to public health laboratories
since 2027, of which there were by then

8
00:00:49,498 --> 00:00:51,767
four hundred thousand on earth.

9
00:00:52,167 --> 00:00:57,974
It came back as nothing. Forty-one targets, all
negative, and an internal control that

10
00:00:57,994 --> 00:00:58,787
was perfect.

11
00:00:59,188 --> 00:01:05,208
A negative on a panel that good, in a patient that
sick, is not an absence. It is a signal.

12
00:01:05,608 --> 00:01:11,798
She pressed the button on the instrument that nobody
ever presses: *report anomalous negative.*

13
00:01:12,197 --> 00:01:18,439
That button had been in the firmware since 2028.
Wesker had specified it personally, over

14
00:01:18,459 --> 00:01:23,927
the objection of two product managers who said
no clinician would ever use it.

15
00:01:24,328 --> 00:01:27,888
It rang a desk in Singapore at four
minutes past midnight.

16
00:01:28,287 --> 00:01:34,800
The narrator would like to describe Umbrella's
pandemic response as it actually was in

17
00:01:34,820 --> 00:01:38,446
2033, because the world only ever
saw the tents.

18
00:01:38,846 --> 00:01:45,008
Four hundred thousand instruments in a hundred and
sixty countries, all reporting. A cold

19
00:01:45,028 --> 00:01:50,912
chain with six hundred vehicles and forty-one
aircraft. Nine hundred million doses of

20
00:01:50,932 --> 00:01:56,886
a dozen things sitting in warehouses at Umbrella's
own expense, in four of the largest

21
00:01:56,906 --> 00:02:02,096
economies on earth, under contracts that paid
nothing until they were used.

22
00:02:02,495 --> 00:02:09,274
And a sequencing turnaround, from a swab anywhere on
earth to a published genome, of nineteen

23
00:02:09,294 --> 00:02:09,712
hours.

24
00:02:10,112 --> 00:02:15,741
The World Health Organization's was eleven days.
That is not incompetence. That is the

25
00:02:15,761 --> 00:02:21,456
difference between an institution built of member
states and a company built by one man

26
00:02:21,476 --> 00:02:26,383
who had asked, four years earlier, what the
answer would need to look like.

27
00:02:26,782 --> 00:02:32,577
The genome was public at six o'clock on the evening
of the fifth of March, thirty-one

28
00:02:32,597 --> 00:02:37,092
hours before the Italian health ministry
formally notified anyone.

29
00:02:37,492 --> 00:02:43,594
Wesker had flown in overnight. He stood at the back
of the operations room and watched

30
00:02:43,614 --> 00:02:49,787
a cluster in Emilia-Romagna go from fourteen cases
to two hundred and forty in nineteen

31
00:02:49,807 --> 00:02:54,769
hours, and he did something that his
executives had never seen him do.

32
00:02:55,169 --> 00:02:56,899
He let other people run it.

33
00:02:57,419 --> 00:03:03,256
Dr. Voss has operational command. Nobody asks me for
a decision unless the decision is

34
00:03:03,276 --> 00:03:06,388
about money, and Ms. Adeyemi may
answer those.

35
00:03:06,788 --> 00:03:09,136
You are the most capable person
in this building.

36
00:03:09,537 --> 00:03:14,318
I am the most capable person in this building at
eleven things, and this is not one of

37
00:03:14,338 --> 00:03:18,227
them. I have never in my life run a
containment. I have *broken* four.

38
00:03:18,901 --> 00:03:24,959
The narrator notes that this is the only occasion
in sixty-five years on which Albert

39
00:03:24,979 --> 00:03:31,036
Wesker declined to take charge of something, and
that it is also, not coincidentally,

40
00:03:31,056 --> 00:03:35,611
the single greatest source of public
goodwill he ever generated.

41
00:03:36,012 --> 00:03:40,461
The tents went up in the Piazza del Popolo
on the morning of the ninth.

42
00:03:40,861 --> 00:03:46,735
White, with a crimson iris on the roof panels —
placed there, deliberately, so that every

43
00:03:46,755 --> 00:03:52,429
aerial shot would carry it — and staffed by four
hundred and eleven Umbrella personnel

44
00:03:52,449 --> 00:03:56,932
who had been in the air within nine hours
of the anomalous negative.

45
00:03:57,331 --> 00:04:03,593
By the twelfth there were tents in six Italian cities,
in Ljubljana, in Zagreb, in Munich.

46
00:04:03,613 --> 00:04:09,734
By the nineteenth there were six hundred sites in
thirty-one countries, and Umbrella had

47
00:04:09,754 --> 00:04:15,736
run nine million tests, free, without asking any
government for permission and without

48
00:04:15,756 --> 00:04:17,201
being refused by any.

49
00:04:17,601 --> 00:04:23,905
The antiviral came out of the Epic blueprint line he
had pulled in 2027 — the one whose

50
00:04:23,925 --> 00:04:30,447
panel had said *this item exists because you will need
it* — and Birkin's people at Arklay

51
00:04:30,467 --> 00:04:33,209
matched it to the genome in four days.

52
00:04:33,610 --> 00:04:39,644
The narrator will say this plainly: it was a real
medicine, it worked, it was manufactured

53
00:04:39,663 --> 00:04:43,680
at sixteen plants on four continents,
and it was given away.

54
00:04:44,079 --> 00:04:47,530
Total deaths, worldwide: four
thousand one hundred.

55
00:04:47,930 --> 00:04:54,523
The modelling groups' central estimate for an outbreak of
that genome and that transmissibility,

56
00:04:54,543 --> 00:04:59,760
under the 2019 international system, was
between two and twenty-one million.

57
00:05:00,160 --> 00:05:03,360
It ended on the fourth of May.
Sixty-one days.

58
00:05:03,879 --> 00:05:08,362
(broadcast) Sixty-one days. I want to say that
again, because eleven years ago we lived

59
00:05:08,382 --> 00:05:13,175
through the other version of this. Sixty-one days from
first cluster to declared containment,

60
00:05:13,195 --> 00:05:15,969
and the organisation that did it was
not a government.

61
00:05:16,370 --> 00:05:21,104
(broadcast) The WHO's regional director this morning
called Umbrella's response "the finest

62
00:05:21,124 --> 00:05:25,807
piece of public health logistics in the history of the
field," and then spent nine minutes

63
00:05:25,827 --> 00:05:28,420
being asked why his own
organisation didn't do it.

64
00:05:28,819 --> 00:05:30,730
(broadcast) Did he have an answer?

65
00:05:31,129 --> 00:05:32,706
(broadcast) He had a budget.

66
00:05:33,105 --> 00:05:39,563
In Ravenna, the tents came down on the eighth of May,
and the city council voted unanimously

67
00:05:39,583 --> 00:05:45,617
to name the square's eastern colonnade after the
company. Wesker declined, in a letter

68
00:05:45,637 --> 00:05:51,602
that was read aloud at the meeting, and asked that
it be named for Dr. Chiara Bonetti

69
00:05:51,622 --> 00:05:53,996
instead, who had pressed a button.

70
00:05:54,396 --> 00:06:00,397
There is a plaque there now. It has her name on it,
and under her name, in smaller letters,

71
00:06:00,417 --> 00:06:06,286
the name of the company that suggested it. The
narrator notes that this cost him nothing,

72
00:06:06,305 --> 00:06:11,976
that he thought of it in about four seconds, and
that it is quoted in every profile of

73
00:06:11,996 --> 00:06:14,225
him written between 2033 and 2050.

74
00:06:15,375 --> 00:06:22,832
Mission: Indispensable. Outbreak contained in
sixty-one days. Lives saved, modelled central

75
00:06:22,852 --> 00:06:29,899
estimate: three million nine hundred thousand.
Global sentiment: eighty-eight per cent

76
00:06:29,919 --> 00:06:36,882
favourable, the highest ever recorded for a private
entity. Two million Points. Note:

77
00:06:36,902 --> 00:06:41,894
you have not lied, coerced or killed to
obtain this position.

78
00:06:42,485 --> 00:06:44,213
Was that admiration?

79
00:06:44,612 --> 00:06:51,370
It was an observation. You have been offered four
hundred opportunities to do this the

80
00:06:51,390 --> 00:06:58,227
other way since 2026. You have declined all of them,
and you are further ahead than any

81
00:06:58,247 --> 00:06:59,882
host I have measured.

82
00:07:00,281 --> 00:07:01,331
Any *host*.

83
00:07:02,117 --> 00:07:03,760
The panel is accurate.

84
00:07:04,160 --> 00:07:07,090
He asked about that one for the next
thirty years too.

85
00:07:07,490 --> 00:07:13,140
The narrator would like to put you inside one of the
tents, because the rest of this chapter

86
00:07:13,160 --> 00:07:15,360
is logistics and the tents were not.

87
00:07:15,759 --> 00:07:21,336
Umbrella's field teams were a mixture: about a third
Absolute, about a third integrated

88
00:07:21,356 --> 00:07:27,319
staff from the plants, and about a third ordinary
recruits who had answered an advertisement.

89
00:07:27,339 --> 00:07:32,852
They did not wear rank. They worked seven-hour
shifts and were rotated out at fourteen

90
00:07:32,872 --> 00:07:38,771
days without exception, which was a rule Wesker had
written personally in 2029 after reading

91
00:07:38,791 --> 00:07:44,368
the occupational literature on aid work, and which
the medical directors had fought and

92
00:07:44,388 --> 00:07:44,689
lost.

93
00:07:45,089 --> 00:07:51,770
A nurse from Bologna named Paola Rinaldi worked four
rotations in that outbreak and gave

94
00:07:51,790 --> 00:07:58,546
an interview in 2041 that is the most-quoted
first-hand account of the Umbrella response.

95
00:07:59,065 --> 00:08:03,464
They were not kind, exactly. That is the thing
people get wrong. They were *competent*,

96
00:08:03,484 --> 00:08:07,882
at a level I had never seen, and competence at that
level looks like coldness until you

97
00:08:07,902 --> 00:08:09,254
have been in it for a week.

98
00:08:09,535 --> 00:08:14,271
The supplies arrived before we asked. The rota was
correct. Nobody ever told me something

99
00:08:14,291 --> 00:08:18,813
would be fixed and then it was not fixed. In
nineteen years of hospital work that had

100
00:08:18,833 --> 00:08:20,203
never once happened to me.

101
00:08:20,812 --> 00:08:25,041
(very slow) And there was a man in my tent on the
fourth night, in a black coat, at about

102
00:08:25,061 --> 00:08:29,529
three in the morning, who spent two hours moving oxygen
cylinders because we were short-handed

103
00:08:29,549 --> 00:08:31,629
and a delivery had gone to the
wrong square.

104
00:08:31,910 --> 00:08:36,345
I did not know who he was. He did not say anything
except to ask where they went. I found

105
00:08:36,365 --> 00:08:40,600
out four days later, from a photograph, and I have
thought about it ever since, and I

106
00:08:40,620 --> 00:08:44,955
still cannot tell you whether that was a man helping
or a man making sure the cylinders

107
00:08:44,975 --> 00:08:46,057
got to the right tent.

108
00:08:46,456 --> 00:08:52,120
The narrator has checked the logistics record. The
delivery did go to the wrong square.

109
00:08:52,140 --> 00:08:57,803
Wesker's movements that night are in the
Surveillance Suite log and are consistent with

110
00:08:57,823 --> 00:08:58,586
her account.

111
00:08:58,986 --> 00:09:05,132
He never mentioned it to anybody, it appears in no
Umbrella publication, and the narrator

112
00:09:05,152 --> 00:09:11,367
has been unable to determine whether that is modesty
or the most efficient piece of public

113
00:09:11,387 --> 00:09:17,394
relations in the company's history, and suspects
that Albert Wesker could not have told

114
00:09:17,414 --> 00:09:18,157
you either.

115
00:09:18,557 --> 00:09:24,945
Catherine Adeyemi had watched the whole thing from a
chair beside a telephone, authorising

116
00:09:24,965 --> 00:09:30,997
nineteen billion dollars of expenditure that no
contract would ever reimburse, and on

117
00:09:31,017 --> 00:09:36,337
the last night she asked him the question she
had been holding since March.

118
00:09:36,857 --> 00:09:41,712
Nineteen billion. Free antiviral, free testing, four
hundred and eleven aircraft movements,

119
00:09:41,732 --> 00:09:46,319
nine thousand staff in the field for two months. We
have taken the largest single-year

120
00:09:46,339 --> 00:09:50,550
loss in the history of commerce and the share
price has gone up sixty per cent.

121
00:09:50,951 --> 00:09:51,573
Yes.

122
00:09:51,971 --> 00:09:53,168
You planned that.

123
00:09:53,567 --> 00:09:58,616
I planned the instruments in 2027 and the button in
2028 and the stockpile clause in 2030

124
00:09:58,636 --> 00:10:03,514
and the blueprint line came out of a lottery I did
not control. I did not plan a virus

125
00:10:03,534 --> 00:10:08,811
in Ravenna. I *prepared* for one, for six years, at
enormous expense, while every institution

126
00:10:08,831 --> 00:10:12,798
that was supposed to be preparing was
writing reports about preparing.

127
00:10:13,197 --> 00:10:15,168
They walked in silence for a while.

128
00:10:15,687 --> 00:10:18,966
I've been trying for four years to decide
whether you are a monster.

129
00:10:19,368 --> 00:10:19,942
And?

130
00:10:20,341 --> 00:10:25,045
I think you might be the most useful person who has
ever lived, and I think that is not

131
00:10:25,065 --> 00:10:29,388
the same question, and I think the second
question is the one that frightens me.

132
00:10:29,787 --> 00:10:32,076
Wesker stopped at the window.

133
00:10:32,596 --> 00:10:34,311
Ask it properly.

134
00:10:34,711 --> 00:10:39,123
Three million nine hundred thousand people are alive
because of what you built. That is

135
00:10:39,143 --> 00:10:43,555
not rhetoric, it's in the modelling, I have read it
four times. And every single one of

136
00:10:43,575 --> 00:10:47,937
them now lives in a world where one man can do that,
and therefore one man can decline

137
00:10:47,957 --> 00:10:48,090
to.

138
00:10:48,809 --> 00:10:53,228
You didn't save them from a virus. You saved them
from a *system*, and then you replaced

139
00:10:53,248 --> 00:10:54,086
it with yourself.

140
00:10:54,485 --> 00:11:00,342
The narrator asks you to notice that Albert Wesker
did not deny it, did not smile, and

141
00:11:00,362 --> 00:11:06,697
did not threaten her. He looked out over twenty-one
million lights and answered her honestly,

142
00:11:06,717 --> 00:11:10,935
which he did perhaps four times in his
life and always to her.

143
00:11:11,455 --> 00:11:12,102
Yes.

144
00:11:12,501 --> 00:11:16,620
And if you're wrong about something? If you make
one bad decision at that scale?

145
00:11:17,020 --> 00:11:21,783
Then it will be a bad decision made by someone
competent, arriving in nineteen hours,

146
00:11:21,803 --> 00:11:26,735
instead of a good decision made by a committee,
arriving in eleven days. I have run both

147
00:11:26,755 --> 00:11:31,517
experiments now. So has everyone else on this
planet. They watched the first one from

148
00:11:31,537 --> 00:11:33,430
inside their houses for two years.

149
00:11:33,831 --> 00:11:39,680
She did not have an answer. Nobody in the world had
an answer, and that is precisely why

150
00:11:39,700 --> 00:11:42,081
the next decade went the way it did.

151
00:11:43,230 --> 00:11:51,069
Loyalty. Catherine Adeyemi: fifty-four per cent, to
seventy. She is not persuaded. She

152
00:11:51,089 --> 00:11:59,110
has simply run out of alternatives. Those two things
look identical from the outside and

153
00:11:59,130 --> 00:11:59,842
are not.

154
00:12:00,432 --> 00:12:06,387
Four hundred miles away and nine time zones back, a
man stood in a spare room in front

155
00:12:06,407 --> 00:12:07,081
of a wall.

156
00:12:07,481 --> 00:12:14,230
SSA Owen Carrick had been told to close the file in
2029, in 2030 and in 2032. He had

157
00:12:14,250 --> 00:12:21,158
not closed it. He had also not opened a formal
investigation, because he was not a fool

158
00:12:21,178 --> 00:12:25,458
and he knew what the request would
look like on paper.

159
00:12:25,859 --> 00:12:32,006
On the wall: forty-one photographs. Eleven of them
were buildings. One was a page from

160
00:12:32,026 --> 00:12:38,245
a Wilmington planning file with a signature on it.
One was a frame from a Swiss loading

161
00:12:38,265 --> 00:12:44,341
bay, printed at home, the
two-hundred-and-forty-frames-a-second one, the one with the

162
00:12:44,361 --> 00:12:45,058
man in it.

163
00:12:45,458 --> 00:12:51,991
And in the middle, a photograph he had taken himself,
from a public road, of a white six-storey

164
00:12:52,011 --> 00:12:54,198
building on the Christina River.

165
00:12:54,599 --> 00:13:00,356
On the day the world decided Albert Wesker was the
finest thing in it, Owen Carrick stood

166
00:13:00,376 --> 00:13:06,328
in front of that wall and wrote four words on an index
card and pinned it under the picture.

167
00:13:06,846 --> 00:13:08,076
*He is not hiding.*

168
00:13:08,476 --> 00:13:14,181
Underneath, later that night, he added one more
line, and it is the line that ends his

169
00:13:14,201 --> 00:13:16,776
career and begins the rest of his life.

170
00:13:17,297 --> 00:13:18,477
He is *recruiting*.
